WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.280
This is the brief on onboarding to the Azure SPI system.

00:04.820 --> 00:08.860
So this field note breaks down a massive shift in OSDU,

00:09.320 --> 00:12.200
the open subsurface data universe for energy data,

00:12.420 --> 00:16.100
and reveals exactly how Microsoft rebuilt its SPI,

00:16.440 --> 00:18.020
or software provider interfaces,

00:18.520 --> 00:21.520
so engineers can seamlessly integrate community code

00:21.520 --> 00:23.440
with real-world Azure infrastructure.

00:24.040 --> 00:26.180
First, we've got the great code base split.

00:26.660 --> 00:29.940
You know, the community stopped keeping everyone's cloud-specific code

00:29.940 --> 00:31.280
in one giant repository.

00:31.900 --> 00:33.600
Now they just maintain the shared core,

00:33.840 --> 00:37.000
and Microsoft completely owns the Azure-specific code.

00:37.420 --> 00:39.020
It's kind of like a car, right?

00:39.420 --> 00:41.440
The community builds the universal chassis,

00:41.860 --> 00:43.660
but Microsoft is solely on the hook

00:43.660 --> 00:46.260
for building the custom Azure engine that drops into it.

00:46.760 --> 00:49.400
Now, that split necessitates a daily sync system.

00:50.060 --> 00:52.860
Second, because that community chassis is constantly updating,

00:53.260 --> 00:55.020
the Azure code lives in service forks

00:55.020 --> 00:57.160
that pull in new shared code every single day.

00:57.740 --> 00:58.880
So how do you keep the community

00:58.880 --> 01:01.580
from accidentally deleting the Azure code during these syncs?

01:02.100 --> 01:04.040
Well, the system generates branches daily

01:04.040 --> 01:06.360
that deliberately exclude the Azure provider,

01:06.800 --> 01:08.020
creating structural protection

01:08.020 --> 01:10.600
so nobody has to constantly rescue deleted files.

01:10.960 --> 01:14.060
And naturally, that sync system requires the actual testing stack.

01:14.540 --> 01:15.840
Finally, the proving ground.

01:16.260 --> 01:19.500
Look, a successful build doesn't mean the code actually works on Azure.

01:20.040 --> 01:22.520
Engineers use the stack to spin up a disposable,

01:22.980 --> 01:24.160
real Azure environment,

01:24.160 --> 01:27.140
using real services like Cosmos DB and Key Vault

01:27.140 --> 01:30.120
to run a borrow, prove, and restore testing cycle.

01:30.860 --> 01:31.420
You might ask,

01:31.740 --> 01:33.700
why not just use fake simulated services

01:33.700 --> 01:35.120
to save an hour of spin-up time?

01:35.540 --> 01:38.160
Well, because testing Azure code on non-Azure substitutes

01:38.160 --> 01:41.380
completely bypasses the very code the environment exists to prove.

01:41.380 --> 01:43.700
You absolutely have to use the real deal.

01:44.440 --> 01:47.140
Ultimately, Azure SPI is the critical bridge

01:47.140 --> 01:49.460
connecting fast-moving open-source innovation

01:49.460 --> 01:51.940
with hard, dependable cloud reality.

